    <edge source="paper-0022" target="paper-0022::claim-01" id="e000958">
      <data key="d6">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d7">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d8">Abstract: 'MBSE involves creating a system model that is a single source for system engineering such as architecture development and interface management. The system model also integrates other discipline specific engineering models and simulations.' Figure 5: 'Model updates are automatically populated into the system views.'</data>
      <data key="d9">medium</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0022" target="paper-0022::claim-02" id="e000969">
      <data key="d6">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d7">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d8">Goal: 'Provide a CubeSat Reference Model that CubeSat teams can use as starting point for their mission-specific CubeSat model.' Section III.A: 'provide a logical architecture... the building blocks for any kind of CubeSat mission.'</data>
      <data key="d9">medium</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0022" target="paper-0022::claim-03" id="e000979">
      <data key="d6">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d7">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d8">Section III.C: 'First, the MOEs are decomposed into measures of performances (MOPs) and technical performance measures (TPMs).' 'The interface supports the input (driving parameters) into the space system model and the output (quantifying parameters) from the space system model.'</data>
      <data key="d9">medium</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0022" target="paper-0022::claim-04" id="e000989">
      <data key="d6">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d7">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d8">Section III: 'The following two trade studies were demonstrated: 1) On board energy level as a function of solar panel area and maximum battery capacity, and 2) quantity of data downloaded...' Section III.C: 'The model is used to establish and evaluate a second-order trade space that provides sufficient power for collect and downlink mission data...'</data>
      <data key="d9">medium</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0022" target="paper-0022::claim-05" id="e000998">
      <data key="d6">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d7">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d8">'Providing a fully-formed CubeSat Reference Model could be a bit overwhelming... Having models for each life-cycle phase is a more gradual way to get up to speed. That should also make it easier for a team to modify, add, and remove SysML elements...'</data>
      <data key="d9">medium</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0022" target="paper-0022::claim-06" id="e001005">
      <data key="d6">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d7">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d8">'MBSE holds the promise of reducing the burden of systems engineering tasks... a properly designed reference model can serve as a checklist... and promote uniformity and consistency across future CubeSat models.'</data>
      <data key="d9">high</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0022" target="paper-0022::claim-07" id="e001016">
      <data key="d6">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d7">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d8">Section III.B: 'The SSWG team is developing a model distribution process to share to external entities...' Figure 9 lists 'MagicDraw, ZIP File, XMI File, OpenMBEE, and Teamcenter.' Conclusion: team 'hopes to release the models publicly in the near future.'</data>
      <data key="d9">medium</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0023" target="paper-0023::claim-01" id="e001025">
      <data key="d6">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d7">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d8">Section 2: "The aim of using MBSE for a nanosatellite project is to create a model framework or repository that can be used by university teams (with limited resources) wishing to design CubeSat or Nanosatellite projects. This type of formal modeling application will guide them through the entire nanosatellite mission design and management process (mission analysis, system design verification, and validation)." Section 3.2/Fig. 4: the authors adopt "an MBSE Framework approach based on a model comprising a consistent set of views reflecting multiple viewpoints and stages in the nanosatellite project"; Fig. 4 shows Mission Analysis, System Formal Design, System Production &amp; Integration, and System Deployment &amp; Operation views.</data>
      <data key="d9">high</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0023" target="paper-0023::claim-02" id="e001035">
      <data key="d6">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d7">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d8">Section 3.1: "The project management procedures proposed by standards like ECSS are sometimes too over-specified for nanosatellite projects which are usually undertaken by universities or academic institutions." Section 3.2: academic nanosatellite specificities allow simplifying the structure: requirements are derived from mission objectives; budget is allocated to hardware/software; "schedule and Coast Breakdown Structure can be directly derived from the Product Tree"; "students-driven projects" have changing personnel and "Business Agreement Structure can be neglected." Section 3.4: outputs for the Formal Design Phase include "Product breakdown structure (PBS)", "Work breakdown structure (WBS)", and "Cost breakdown structure (CBS)"; CBS costs are primarily hardware/software development and testing for student-driven projects.</data>
      <data key="d9">high</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0023" target="paper-0023::claim-03" id="e001045">
      <data key="d6">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d7">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d8">Section 1: "The combination of Model-Based Systems Engineering (MBSE) and Systems Modeling Language (SysML) emerged as an alternative management and design approach that improves mission reliability ... Typical advantages of MBSE are that it provides better traceability, design consistency, quality improvement, and reduced document management overhead."</data>
      <data key="d9">high</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0023" target="paper-0023::claim-04" id="e001054">
      <data key="d6">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d7">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d8">Section 5.1: "requirements tables were generated ... These tables describe the requirements and their relationship to other SysML design elements to facilitate requirements traceability, satisfaction, and verification." Section 5.1/Fig. 15: "The "Satisfied By" column shows the model elements that fulfill the requirement. For example, the "Power System" subsystem must satisfy operational requirement 100.1.6."; "The 'Verified By' column shows the specific test cases that determine how the system meets the requirement ... "Bus System Activation" activity diagram ... is used to verify operational requirement 100.1.6." Section 5.1/Fig. 16: "The requirements matrix is another important SysML element that can be generated for requirements to design trace"; Fig. 16 shows a Mission Satisfy Requirement Matrix with Power System and Battery/Kill Switch rows.</data>
      <data key="d9">high</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0023" target="paper-0023::claim-05" id="e001064">
      <data key="d6">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d7">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d8">Section 5: "Choosing to use package diagrams at this level of abstract modeling helps stakeholders understand the structure of the model and helps modelers build the model in a more organized way." Section 5/Fig. 11: PBS model elements are "organized ... into packages structured by both SysML diagram type and hierarchy concurrently"; packages include Requirements, Structure, Behavior, Verification, Parametric, and Launch Operation Facility.</data>
      <data key="d9">high</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0023" target="paper-0023::claim-06" id="e001072">
      <data key="d6">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d7">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d8">Section 5.1/Fig. 15: "The "Satisfied By" column shows the model elements that fulfill the requirement. For example, the "Power System" subsystem must satisfy operational requirement 100.1.6."; "The 'Verified By' column shows the specific test cases that determine how the system meets the requirement ... "Bus System Activation" activity diagram ... is used to verify operational requirement 100.1.6." Section 5.3: "The LEOP flow activity diagram is also a way to "verify" functional or operational requirements, such as the Bus System Activation state machine (Fig. 23), which verifies operational requirements 100.1.6." Fig. 23 description: Bus System Activation state machine uses LEOP Safe Mode (do/obc power on), LEOP Critical Mode, and Vbat &gt; Vsafe / Vbat &lt; Vcritic transitions to continue or recover the LEOP flow.</data>
      <data key="d9">medium</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0023" target="paper-0023::claim-07" id="e001081">
      <data key="d6">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d7">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d8">Conclusion: "To improve communication, planning, management, and development, we implemented this approach, replacing ad hoc procedures in the work organization with process flow and model based on the different views of the system architecture." Conclusion: "The proposed modeling also facilitated the development of key documents in a nanosatellite project, namely the product breakdown structure (PBS)" and "the PBS is considered as the backbone of the project design management activities allowing among other things to define the work breakdown structure (WBS) and the cost breakdown structure (CBS)."</data>
      <data key="d9">medium</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0023" target="paper-0023::claim-08" id="e001093">
      <data key="d6">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d7">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d8">Conclusion: "The next step of this work is to improve the viability and practicality of the product breakdown structure model by integrating external analysis and simulation tools such as MATLAB and STK. The aim is to develop an executable SysML model with a graphical user interface to provide insight into how nanosatellites work."</data>
      <data key="d9">high</data>
    </edge>
